---
import ToolLayout from "../../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro";
import { TemperatureConverter } from "../../../components/converters/TemperatureConverter.js";
import { getToolByPath } from "../../../content/tools.js";

const tool = getToolByPath("/tools/converters/temperature")!;
---

<ToolLayout
  tool={tool}
  purpose="Convert Celsius to Fahrenheit, Fahrenheit to Celsius, and either scale to Kelvin."
>
  <TemperatureConverter client:load />

  <Fragment slot="details">
    <h2>Formulas</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><code>°F = (°C × 9/5) + 32</code></li>
      <li><code>K = °C + 273.15</code></li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Common temperatures</h2>
    <table>
      <caption>Temperatures produced by the formulas above</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Celsius</th>
          <th scope="col">Fahrenheit</th>
          <th scope="col">Kelvin</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>0°C</td>
          <td>32°F</td>
          <td>273.15 K</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>20°C</td>
          <td>68°F</td>
          <td>293.15 K</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>37°C</td>
          <td>98.6°F</td>
          <td>310.15 K</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>100°C</td>
          <td>212°F</td>
          <td>373.15 K</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>−40°C</td>
          <td>−40°F</td>
          <td>233.15 K</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      0°C and 100°C are water's freezing and boiling points at standard atmospheric pressure. Related converters:
      <a href="/tools/converters/length">length</a> and <a href="/tools/converters/mass">kilograms to pounds</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Negative values are fully supported for Celsius and Fahrenheit (e.g. −40°C = −40°F).</li>
      <li>Kelvin cannot go below absolute zero (0 K = −273.15°C = −459.67°F). Entering a value that would imply a sub-absolute-zero temperature is rejected rather than silently computed.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Reference</h2>
    <p>The Celsius-Kelvin offset (273.15) and absolute zero are defined by the International System of Units (SI), as maintained by the Bureau International des Poids et Mesures (BIPM) and adopted by NIST.</p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
